   "oldText": "2. For noisy grids containing a distinctive single-color motif amid zeros and another background color, record the motif's colored-cell offsets from its bounding box. Search for translated copies whose motif positions all contain zeros, recoloring those matching positions with the motif color. Preserve every other cell, including the original motif and zeros/background at non-motif positions inside the bounding box. Do not assume flood-fill or neighbor propagation: test motif translations when the grid visually consists of noisy background plus one seed object.\n3. Use a small script to enumerate translations and construct output grids when manual scanning is error-prone. Validate by matching all demonstrated changed/unchanged cells; account for seed shape, legal translations within bounds, and any rotation/reflection only if supported by demonstrations.\n4. When full-width and full-height separator lines partition a grid into equal tiles and the output dimensions match the tile layout, map each tile to one output cell. Use demonstrations to identify which in-tile feature or count determines the output; do not assume every unusual cell or single marker is positive. For the task pattern with color-8 separators, 2×3 tiles containing mostly color 3 and occasional color 2, emit 1 for a tile with at least two 2s and 3 otherwise.\n",
